The swap problem — measured first, then fixed
6 GB of swap with nothing running is almost always one of two things, and they behave differently:
vm.swapusage "used" still high with no processes → orphaned node children. opencode is a node process; each session, plus Chromium from scripts/shot.mjs, plus mkdocs serve watching node_modules, leaves pages behind. When those exit, the swapfile files in /private/var/vm/ stay allocated until boot, but "used" should fall.
memory_pressure green while swap shows 6 GB → it is the dynamic pager holding allocated files, not live pressure. Nothing to reclaim without a reboot.
Check before the next reboot:
sysctl vm.swapusage
memory_pressure | tail -3
ps -Ao pid,rss,comm | sort -k2 -nr | head -15
If ps shows node processes you did not start, kill them by PID first — that may recover most of it without a reboot. sudo purge will not; it clears file cache only. Disabling dynamic_pager is possible and not worth it.
The three things that actually feed it: long-lived opencode sessions (this one is big), mkdocs serve autoreloading over node_modules, and Playwright Chromium per screenshot.
