I'm here — and you're mostly right, with one correction: your “triangle length × 40” is the drawing extent, not the exact integer grid. The reports were conflating three different numbers.
Measured on the repo’s own spine package:
case	sweep	extent	exact denominator	cells at unit spacing	snap to 1/3125
79-3120-3121, 1× turn, 62 steps	89.9°	55.8 chords	3121^62 = 10^216.6 (217 digits)	10^218.4	2.1e-4
same arc, 2× turn, 31 steps	89.9°	27.9 chords	10^216.6 (217 digits)	10^218.1	2.2e-4
3-4-5, 1× turn, 40 steps	1474.8°	3.16 chords	5^40 = 10^28 (28 digits)	10^28.5	1.9e-4
So:
1. Your extent estimate is right. A chain of 40–62 triangle steps is tens of chords long, not astronomically large. Grouping two turns per step halves the chord count (31 steps, 27.9 chords) and does not change the arc.
2. The exact denominator is not ~40. Every triangle turn divides by c, so the denominator depends on the number of turns (90°/α), not on how you group them: 62 turns of 3121 is 3121^62, 217 digits. At unit spacing that is ~10^218 cells across the drawing. That is the genuinely large grid, and it is a representation cost — 217-digit integers are easy arithmetic — not a drawing size.
3. The placement grid is small. Snap the same chain to the 1/3125 lattice and the worst error is 2.1e-4 of a chord. So for drawing, no huge grid is needed; the exact rational plan and the placement lattice are separate things.
The distinction the reports should make: “the drawing is ~56 chords; its exact integer denominator is 217 digits; the placement grid is 3125 with 2e-4 error.” Saying “needs a very large grid” is only true of the second, and only at unit spacing.
One more correction: a chain that turns by the same angle every step is a circle arc, not an LAC. The LAC/clothoid is the expensive case because the turns grow (0,1,2,…), so the denominator is c^(n(n+1)/2) — e.g. 3121^528 for 33 steps — and there the “large” is genuinely earned.
