 both setups are now in the notes with their own figures.
Is m = 1.2 an elastica a circle? No — that was the bug.
The free elastica minimises ∫κ²ds: κ″ + ½κ³ = 0, first integral κ′² + κ⁴/4 = C. With maximum curvature m, C = m⁴/4, so κ′ = ±½√(m⁴−κ⁴). Curvature runs from 0 to ±m; the curve inflects wherever κ = 0.
A circle has constant κ ≠ 0, which does not satisfy that equation (only κ = 0 does). A circle is a solution of the constrained elastica, κ″ + ½κ³ = λκ with λ = κ²/2 — a different member.
The first version started the integrator at κ = m, where κ′ = 0. It never left, so κ stayed constant and the curve was a circle of radius 1/m. That is exactly what you saw for m = 1.2.
Started correctly at the inflection (κ = 0), m = 1.2 is a shallow wave, not a circle — that corrected curve is the figure in the new note. Larger m gives deeper waves and loops; the panels now use 2.2, 3.0, 3.8.
The two notes
How to set up an elastica correctly — the ODE, the first integral, the start point and turning points, why starting at m is the trap, m as curvature amplitude, and the fact that a G2 elastica fit (solving m, λ, rotation, scale from end data) is not implemented — the page draws the family only.
How to set up a G2 quintic Hermite span — the six data per span, the basis identities p′(0)=v₀, p″(0)=a₀, the h and h² scaling when spans have different parameter lengths, and the C2-versus-G2 distinction: matching p″ as vectors is stronger than matching curvature, but copying p″ without the h² factor breaks the join.
